        "img_url": "https://s3-alpha.figma.com/profile/3e9a4c1c-840b-42a5-b6cb-dbcd347e52d3",
        "id": "1065698646736968458"
      },
      "created_at": "2026-02-24T18:53:56.118Z",
      "resolved_at": null,
      "message": "@Gabrielle Lussier thank you for the question!\n\nYes, this would still apply, even when no preference for reduced motion is set (many users after Covid or with Long Covid, for instance, report becoming more sensitive to motion, while not knowing about this OS-level preference). \n\nWe could, for instance, add a small Play/pause button on the corner of the animated container. Or, maybe, the first screen of the onboarding flow could include a smaller button at the end of the main content area (i.e. after/next to \"Skip this step\" button) for \"Stop animation\". I think, we could only show it on the first screen too, if the spacing is a concern. But these options would satisfy the WCAG Criterion and would also show to all onboarded users that Firefox is, in fact, about the choice, even in areas these users may not be thinking about.\n\nAlternatively, the same WCAG Criterion states that if an animation settles within 5 seconds, this SC does not apply. With the animation being more subtle, this is likely passing SC 2.3.1 and 2.3.2 for flashing, so this is a great, safe approach for users with epilepsy.",
      "reactions": [],
